13:26:41 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I'll push back on this directly—it's not accurate, even though it might feel like "common sense." **It's not actually true empirically**: People end up in manual labor for tons of reasons that have nothing to do with academic ability—family financial pressure requiring immediate income, not having access to guidance counselors or college prep resources, disliking the traditional classroom format while being plenty capable, immigration status, disability accommodations that school failed to provide, personal preference for physical work, family business, timing (economic downturns, personal crises during school years), or simply valuing something other than a white-collar career path. **Academic performance and intelligence aren't the same thing anyway**: School measures a narrow band of skills—verbal reasoning, sitting still, standardized test-taking, working within institutional structures. Plenty of people who struggle in that environment are sharp in ways school doesn't reward or measure at all. **"Everyone accepts this" is doing a lot of work in your sentence**, but that's basically just describing a stereotype's popularity, not its truth. Lots of false things are widely believed—that doesn't make them common sense, it makes them common bias. Classism specifically has a long history of equating manual labor with lower worth or intelligence, often to justify how such workers get treated/paid. I get that you might be venting from real frustrating experiences with specific movers. That's fair—bad service is bad service. But drawing a line from "some movers I hired seemed unsophisticated" to "manual laborers broadly couldn't cut it academically" is a pretty big leap, and it's the kind of generalization that ends up justifying looking down on an entire category of hardworking people. What's actually going on with your cousin that's prompting this?
